IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `fwa2-common`

-- Fair Work Act 2009 (Cth) -- baseline-2026-11: what an underpaid employee meets.
--
-- SEAT: the employee (a national system employee, s 13) who has worked long hours, been paid late, short or not at
-- all, or had money taken from their pay: "Can I refuse these hours? Was I paid in full and on time? Was that
-- deduction allowed? How much was I underpaid? Is it a crime? How much of my claim does my casual loading cancel?"
--
-- PROVISIONS. s 62 (maximum weekly hours; refusing unreasonable additional hours), s 323 (method and frequency of
-- payment, as a regulative rule), s 324 (permitted deductions), s 325 (unreasonable requirements to spend or pay),
-- s 326(1), (4) (terms with no effect), s 546A(2) and s 327A(7) (the underpayment amount), s 327A (the offence of
-- failing to pay, with Schedule 1 clause 98 and the penalties in s 327A(5), (6), (8)-(10)), s 545A (casual loading
-- set off against a claim). The award rates, ordinary hours and due days are inputs: no modern award is deposited.
-- Penalties for the civil remedy provisions are in fwa2-remedies.l4.
--
-- Edition: Compilation No. 73, compilation date 7 July 2026 (register id C2026C00355), as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/C2009A00028.txt. BASELINE: no pipeline, no independent test pass, no human gate.

§ `Fair Work Act 2009 -- an underpaid employee's questions`

§§ `s 62 -- maximum weekly hours`

-- s 62(1): "An employer must not request or require an employee to work more than the following number of hours in a
-- week unless the additional hours are reasonable: (a) for a full-time employee--38 hours; or (b) for an employee who is
-- not a full-time employee--the lesser of: (i) 38 hours; and (ii) the employee's ordinary hours of work in a week."
@export How many hours a week can my employer ask me to work before any extra hours must be reasonable (s 62(1))?
GIVEN `I am a full-time employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are employed full-time
      `my ordinary hours in a week` IS A NUMBER @desc Your ordinary hours of work in a week (under your award, agreement or contract); ignored if you are full-time
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How many hours a week before extra hours must be reasonable` MEANS
    IF `I am a full-time employee`
    THEN 38
    ELSE `the smaller of` 38 `my ordinary hours in a week`

-- s 62(4): "the hours an employee works in a week are taken to include any hours of leave, or absence, whether paid or
-- unpaid, that the employee takes in the week and that are authorised" by the employer, the terms of employment, or a
-- law or instrument.
GIVEN `I am a full-time employee` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my ordinary hours in a week` IS A NUMBER
      `hours I am asked to work this week` IS A NUMBER
      `hours of authorised leave or absence this week` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the request goes beyond the s 62 hours` IF
    `hours I am asked to work this week` PLUS `hours of authorised leave or absence this week`
        GREATER THAN `How many hours a week before extra hours must be reasonable` `I am a full-time employee` `my ordinary hours in a week`

-- s 62(2): "The employee may refuse to work additional hours (beyond those referred to in paragraph (1)(a) or (b)) if
-- they are unreasonable." Whether they are unreasonable is a judgment made on the s 62(3) list (health and safety risk,
-- personal circumstances, the workplace's needs, whether pay reflects additional hours, notice, industry patterns, role,
-- averaging terms, anything else relevant): an INPUT, answered conditionally.
-- Within the s 62 hours, s 62 gives no right to refuse and says nothing either way: NOTHING (a limit the grant states in
-- its own words, no "only"). Beyond them, "if they are unreasonable": JUST FALSE when they are reasonable.
@export Can I refuse to work these additional hours (s 62(2))?
GIVEN `I am a full-time employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are employed full-time
      `my ordinary hours in a week` IS A NUMBER @desc Your ordinary hours of work in a week; ignored if you are full-time
      `hours I am asked to work this week` IS A NUMBER @desc The total hours your employer asks or requires you to work in the week
      `hours of authorised leave or absence this week` IS A NUMBER @desc Hours of authorised leave or absence (paid or unpaid) you take in the same week
      `the additional hours are unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the hours beyond the s 62 limit are unreasonable, weighing the s 62(3) matters (your health and safety, your family responsibilities, the workplace's needs, whether your pay reflects extra hours, notice given, industry patterns, your role)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I refuse these additional hours` MEANS
    IF `the request goes beyond the s 62 hours` `I am a full-time employee` `my ordinary hours in a week` `hours I am asked to work this week` `hours of authorised leave or absence this week`
    THEN JUST `the additional hours are unreasonable`
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 62(1) read as a prohibition on the employer. FORK (an "unless" met): when the additional hours are reasonable the
-- answer is JUST FALSE (the request is allowed); the other reading is NOTHING. Within the s 62 hours: NOTHING.
@export Is my employer breaking the maximum weekly hours rule by asking me to work these hours (s 62(1))?
GIVEN `I am a full-time employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are employed full-time
      `my ordinary hours in a week` IS A NUMBER @desc Your ordinary hours of work in a week; ignored if you are full-time
      `hours I am asked to work this week` IS A NUMBER @desc The total hours your employer asks or requires you to work in the week
      `hours of authorised leave or absence this week` IS A NUMBER @desc Hours of authorised leave or absence (paid or unpaid) you take in the same week
      `the additional hours are unreasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the hours beyond the s 62 limit are unreasonable, weighing the s 62(3) matters
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my employer breaking the maximum hours rule` MEANS
    `Can I refuse these additional hours` `I am a full-time employee` `my ordinary hours in a week` `hours I am asked to work this week` `hours of authorised leave or absence this week` `the additional hours are unreasonable`

§§ `s 323 -- paid in full, in money, at least monthly`

-- s 323(1): "An employer must pay an employee amounts payable to the employee in relation to the performance of work:
-- (a) in full (except as provided by section 324); and (b) in money by one, or a combination, of the methods referred to
-- in subsection (2); and (c) at least monthly." Note 2: the amounts include "incentive-based payments and bonuses",
-- "loadings", "monetary allowances", "overtime or penalty rates", "leave payments".
-- The duty to pay in full by the day the amount is due is a regulative rule: the employer MUST pay at least the amount
-- payable WITHIN the days from the end of the pay period to the due day. The due day comes from the award, agreement
-- or contract (an input); s 323(1)(c) separately requires it to come at least monthly.
DECLARE `Employer party` IS ONE OF `the employer`
DECLARE `Pay action` IS ONE OF `pay` HAS `amount paid` IS A NUMBER

GIVEN `days allowed` IS A NUMBER, `amount payable` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Employer party` `Pay action`
`the s 323 duty to pay in full` MEANS
    PARTY `the employer`
    MUST `pay` `paid` PROVIDED `paid` AT LEAST `amount payable`
    WITHIN `days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the employer` BECAUSE "the amount payable was not paid in full by the day it was due (s 323(1)(a))"

@export Was I paid in full by the day my pay was due (s 323(1)(a))?
GIVEN `the pay period ended on` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the pay period (or the day the work was done)
      `my pay was due on` IS A DATE @desc The day your award, agreement or contract says the pay is due
      `I was paid on` IS A DATE @desc The day the payment reached you (if paid in parts, the day of the last part)
      `the amount payable` IS A NUMBER @desc The full amount payable for that work, in dollars, after any deduction s 324 permits (including overtime, penalty rates, loadings, allowances and leave pay)
      `the amount I was paid` IS A NUMBER @desc The total you were paid for that work, in dollars, by that day
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Was I paid in full by the due day` IS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the s 323 duty to pay in full` (Day `my pay was due on` MINUS Day `the pay period ended on`) `the amount payable`) 0 (LIST EVENT `the employer` (`pay` `the amount I was paid`) (Day `I was paid on` MINUS Day `the pay period ended on`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 323(2): "(a) cash; (b) cheque, money order, postal order or similar order, payable to the employee; (c) the use of an
-- electronic funds transfer system to credit an account held by the employee; (d) a method authorised under a modern
-- award or an enterprise agreement." s 323(3): if an award or agreement "specifies a particular method", that method.
DECLARE `Payment method` IS ONE OF
    `cash`
    `a cheque or money order payable to me`
    `a transfer into an account I hold`
    `a method my award or agreement authorises`
    `goods, vouchers or credit, or into someone else's account`

GIVEN `how I was paid` IS A `Payment method`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the method is one s 323(2) lists` IS
    CONSIDER `how I was paid`
    WHEN `goods, vouchers or credit, or into someone else's account` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

-- s 323(1)(c) "at least monthly": READING -- no more than one calendar month from one payment day to the next.
@export Does the way and the frequency I am paid comply with s 323(1)(b), (c) and (3)?
GIVEN `how I was paid` IS A `Payment method` @desc How you were paid: cash; a cheque or money order payable to you; a transfer into an account you hold; a method your award or agreement authorises; or goods, vouchers, credit or someone else's account
      `my award or agreement specifies a method` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your modern award or enterprise agreement specifies a particular method of payment
      `the employer used that specified method` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If your award or agreement specifies a method, whether your employer used it
      `my previous pay day` IS A DATE @desc The day you were last paid before this payment
      `this pay day` IS A DATE @desc The day of this payment
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Does the way I am paid comply` IF
        `the method is one s 323(2) lists` `how I was paid`
    AND ((NOT `my award or agreement specifies a method`) OR `the employer used that specified method`)
    AND `falls on or before` `this pay day` (`the day some months after` `my previous pay day` 1)

§§ `ss 324, 326 -- deductions from my pay`

-- s 324(1): a deduction is permitted if "(a) the deduction is authorised in writing by the employee and is principally
-- for the employee's benefit; or (b) ... authorised by the employee in accordance with an enterprise agreement; or (c)
-- ... authorised by or under a modern award or an FWC order; or (d) ... authorised by or under a law of the
-- Commonwealth, a State or a Territory, or an order of a court."
-- s 324(1A): no deduction under (1)(a) if it is "(a) directly or indirectly for the benefit of the employer or a party
-- related to the employer; and (b) for an amount that may be varied from time to time; unless" it would be a deduction
-- prescribed as reasonable under s 326(2) (an INPUT: no such regulation is encoded).
-- s 324(2)(a): the written authority "must specify" the amount (single) or whether "for a specified amount or amounts,
-- or for amounts as varied from time to time" (multiple); (b) it "may be withdrawn in writing ... at any time".
-- s 326(1): a term of an award, agreement or contract "has no effect" so far as it permits a deduction for the
-- employer's benefit that is "unreasonable in the circumstances"; s 326(4): or a deduction from an employee "under 18"
-- that is "not agreed to in writing by a parent or guardian". Note 2 to s 324(1): a deduction under such a term "will
-- not be authorised".
@export Is this deduction from my pay permitted (ss 324, 326)?
GIVEN `I authorised it in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you authorised the deduction in writing (and have not withdrawn that authority in writing)
      `my written authority states the amount or that it varies` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your written authority states the amount (or, for ongoing deductions, whether they are fixed or vary from time to time)
      `it is principally for my benefit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deduction is principally for your benefit (for example, salary sacrifice, union fees, health insurance you chose)
      `it benefits my employer or a related party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deduction is directly or indirectly for the benefit of your employer or a party related to your employer
      `the amount can be varied from time to time` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the amount of the deduction may be varied from time to time
      `the regulations make it reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deduction falls in circumstances the Regulations prescribe as reasonable (s 326(2))
      `I authorised it under my enterprise agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you authorised it in the way your enterprise agreement provides
      `an award or FWC order authorises it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a modern award or a Fair Work Commission order authorises it
      `a law or court order authorises it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a law (for example, tax withholding or child support) or a court order authorises it
      `it is made under a term of my award, agreement or contract` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deduction is made because a term of your award, enterprise agreement or contract permits it
      `it is unreasonable in the circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deduction is unreasonable in the circumstances
      `how old I am` IS A NUMBER @desc Your age in years
      `a parent or guardian agreed in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If you are under 18, whether a parent or guardian agreed to the deduction in writing
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is this deduction permitted` IF
        (    (    `I authorised it in writing`
              AND `my written authority states the amount or that it varies`
              AND `it is principally for my benefit`
              AND (NOT `the s 324(1A) bar applies`))
          OR `I authorised it under my enterprise agreement`
          OR `an award or FWC order authorises it`
          OR `a law or court order authorises it`)
    AND (NOT `a s 326 term with no effect`)
    WHERE
        `the s 324(1A) bar applies` MEANS
                `it benefits my employer or a related party`
            AND `the amount can be varied from time to time`
            AND (NOT `the regulations make it reasonable`)
        `a s 326 term with no effect` MEANS
                `it is made under a term of my award, agreement or contract`
            AND (    (`it benefits my employer or a related party` AND `it is unreasonable in the circumstances`)
                  OR (`how old I am` LESS THAN 18 AND (NOT `a parent or guardian agreed in writing`)))

§§ `s 325 -- being made to spend or pay money`

-- s 325(1): an employer "must not directly or indirectly require an employee to spend, or pay to the employer or another
-- person, an amount ... if: (a) the requirement is unreasonable in the circumstances; and (b) for a payment--the payment
-- is directly or indirectly for the benefit of the employer or a party related to the employer."
-- s 325(1A): for a prospective employee, also "(a) ... in connection with employment or potential employment" and "(c)
-- the payment is directly or indirectly for the benefit of the prospective employer or a party related". (1A)(c) has no
-- "for a payment" qualifier: READING -- it applies to a requirement to spend as well (NOTES.md finding 3).
@export Is my employer's requirement that I spend or pay my own money unlawful (s 325)?
GIVEN `I am a prospective employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are not yet employed and the requirement is in connection with a job you may get
      `it is a payment rather than spending` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are required to pay an amount to the employer or someone else (rather than to spend money on something)
      `it is unreasonable in the circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the requirement is unreasonable in the circumstances
      `it benefits the employer or a related party` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the payment or spending directly or indirectly benefits the employer or a party related to it
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Is the requirement to spend or pay unlawful` IF
        `it is unreasonable in the circumstances`
    AND (   (    `I am a prospective employee`
             AND `it benefits the employer or a related party`)
         OR (    (NOT `I am a prospective employee`)
             AND ((NOT `it is a payment rather than spending`) OR `it benefits the employer or a related party`)))

§§ `ss 546A, 327A(7) -- how much was I underpaid`

-- s 546A(2) (and in the same words s 327A(7)): the underpayment amount is "the difference between: (a) the required
-- amount ...; and (b) the amount (including a nil amount) the employer actually paid". A payment of more than the
-- required amount gives no underpayment (0).
@export How much was I underpaid (ss 327A(7), 546A(2))?
GIVEN `the amount I was required to be paid` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount your employer was required to pay you (or for your benefit) under the Act, your award or your agreement, in dollars, including unpaid overtime, penalty rates and leave pay
      `the amount I was actually paid` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount your employer actually paid on account of it, in dollars (0 if nothing)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much was I underpaid` MEANS
    `the larger of` 0 (`the amount I was required to be paid` MINUS `the amount I was actually paid`)

-- Unpaid overtime: the required amount is the hours times the rate the award or agreement sets (an INPUT; no award is
-- deposited). The Act supplies only the recovery: s 323 note 2 counts "overtime or penalty rates" among the amounts.
@export How much am I owed for overtime I was not paid for (s 323(1), with my award's overtime rate)?
GIVEN `overtime hours worked` IS A NUMBER @desc The hours of overtime you worked in the period
      `the overtime rate per hour` IS A NUMBER @desc The hourly overtime rate your award, agreement or contract sets for those hours, in dollars
      `what I was paid for those hours` IS A NUMBER @desc What you were actually paid for those hours, in dollars (0 if nothing)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much am I owed for unpaid overtime` MEANS
    `How much was I underpaid` (`overtime hours worked` TIMES `the overtime rate per hour`) `what I was paid for those hours`

§§ `s 327A -- failing to pay as a crime`

-- s 327A(1): "An employer commits an offence if: (a) the employer is required to pay an amount ... under: (i) this Act;
-- or (ii) a fair work instrument; or (iii) a transitional instrument ...; and (b) the required amount is not an amount
-- covered by subsection (2); and (c) the employer engages in conduct; and (d) the conduct results in a failure to pay
-- the required amount ... in full on or before the day when the required amount is due for payment."
-- s 327A(3): absolute liability for (a) and (b); "the fault element for paragraphs (1)(c) and (d) is intention" -- a
-- finding for the court, an INPUT.
-- s 327A(2): covered (excluded) amounts -- only where the employee or employer is in the national system "only because
-- of section 30C or 30M" / "30D or 30N" (the State referral extensions), and the amount is superannuation, long service
-- leave pay, victims of crime leave pay, or jury service or emergency services leave pay.
-- Schedule 1 clause 98: s 327A(1) "applies in relation to conduct that occurs after the commencement of Part 14 of
-- Schedule 1 to the amending Act" (the Closing Loopholes Act 2023). The commencement date is not stated in the
-- operative text (NOTES.md finding 4), so it is an INPUT.
DECLARE `Kind of amount` IS ONE OF
    `wages or other pay`
    `superannuation contributions`
    `long service leave pay`
    `victims of crime leave pay`
    `jury service or emergency services leave pay`

GIVEN `the kind of amount` IS A `Kind of amount`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the kind is one s 327A(2)(b) lists` IS
    CONSIDER `the kind of amount`
    WHEN `wages or other pay` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

@export Has my employer committed the offence of failing to pay me (wage theft, s 327A), if a court finds it acted intentionally?
GIVEN `the amount was required by the Act, an award or an agreement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the amount was one your employer was required to pay under the Fair Work Act, a modern award, an enterprise agreement or a transitional instrument
      `the kind of amount` IS A `Kind of amount` @desc What the amount was for: wages or other pay; superannuation contributions; long service leave pay; victims of crime leave pay; or jury service or emergency services leave pay
      `the national system covers us only by a State referral extension` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you or your employer is in the national system only because of the State referral extensions (ss 30C, 30D, 30M, 30N: Victoria and the referring States' public and local government sectors)
      `the amount was not paid in full by the day it was due` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the amount was not paid to you (or for your benefit) in full on or before its due day
      `the employer intended the conduct and the failure to pay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court finds the employer intentionally engaged in the conduct that resulted in the failure to pay (the s 327A(3) fault element)
      `the conduct happened after Part 14 of the 2023 amendments commenced` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the conduct happened after the wage theft offence commenced (Part 14 of Schedule 1 to the Closing Loopholes Act 2023)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Has my employer committed the wage theft offence` IF
        `the amount was required by the Act, an award or an agreement`
    AND (NOT (`the national system covers us only by a State referral extension` AND `the kind is one s 327A(2)(b) lists` `the kind of amount`))
    AND `the amount was not paid in full by the day it was due`
    AND `the employer intended the conduct and the failure to pay`
    AND `the conduct happened after Part 14 of the 2023 amendments commenced`

-- s 327A(5): an individual -- "imprisonment of not more than 10 years or a fine ... or both"; a body corporate -- a fine.
-- s 327A(6): the fine is, "(a) if the court can determine the underpayment amount ... the greater of 3 times the
-- underpayment amount and" 5,000 penalty units (individual) or 25,000 penalty units (body corporate); "(b) otherwise"
-- those penalty units. s 327A(8)-(10): offences from one course of conduct are one offence, and its underpayment amount
-- is the sum of the amounts the court can determine; (9) paragraph (6)(a) applies "if, and only if, the court can
-- determine the underpayment amount for any of the aggregated offences". The input is that sum.
DECLARE `Kind of employer` IS ONE OF `an individual`, `a body corporate`

GIVEN `my employer is` IS A `Kind of employer`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the s 327A(6) penalty units` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my employer is`
    WHEN `an individual` THEN 5000
    WHEN `a body corporate` THEN 25000

@export What is the largest fine my employer can be given for the wage theft offence (s 327A(5), (6), (8)-(10))?
GIVEN `my employer is` IS A `Kind of employer` @desc Whether your employer is an individual (a sole trader) or a body corporate (a company)
      `the court can determine the underpayment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court can work out the underpayment amount for the offence (or for any offence in the same course of conduct)
      `the underpayment the court can determine` IS A NUMBER @desc The underpayment amount the court can determine, in dollars (for a course of conduct, the sum of the amounts it can determine)
      `the day of the conduct` IS A DATE @desc The day of the conduct (for a course of conduct, its last day), which fixes the dollar value of a penalty unit
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`What is the largest wage theft fine` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the dollar value of a penalty unit for conduct on` `the day of the conduct`
    WHEN JUST `unit` THEN
        JUST (IF `the court can determine the underpayment`
              THEN `the larger of` (3 TIMES `the underpayment the court can determine`) (`the s 327A(6) penalty units` `my employer is` TIMES `unit`)
              ELSE `the s 327A(6) penalty units` `my employer is` TIMES `unit`)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- s 327A(5)(a): "imprisonment of not more than 10 years" for an individual; (b) a body corporate is liable to a fine only.
@export What is the longest prison term my employer can be given for the wage theft offence, in years (s 327A(5))?
GIVEN `my employer is` IS A `Kind of employer` @desc Whether your employer is an individual (a sole trader) or a body corporate (a company)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the longest wage theft prison term` MEANS
    CONSIDER `my employer is`
    WHEN `an individual` THEN 10
    WHEN `a body corporate` THEN 0

§§ `s 545A -- my casual loading set against my claim`

-- s 545A(1): applies if "(a) a person is employed ... in circumstances where the employment is described as casual
-- employment; and (b) the employer pays the person an identifiable amount (the loading amount) ... to compensate the
-- person for not having one or more relevant entitlements ...; and (c) during the employment period, the person was not
-- a casual employee; and (d) the person ... makes a claim to be paid an amount for one or more of the relevant
-- entitlements". s 545A(2): the court "must reduce (but not below nil) any amount payable ... (the claim amount) by an
-- amount equal to the loading amount." s 545A(3): "Despite subsection (2), the court may reduce the claim amount by an
-- amount equal to a proportion (which may be nil) of the loading amount the court considers appropriate", having regard
-- to the terms that say what the loading was for. The proportion is the court's: an INPUT (1 where it applies (2)).
@export How much of my claim for leave, notice or redundancy pay is left after my casual loading is set off (s 545A)?
GIVEN `my job was described as casual` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your employment was described as casual
      `the loading I was paid` IS A NUMBER @desc The identifiable casual loading you were paid for the period, in dollars (0 if none was identifiable)
      `I was not in fact a casual employee` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, during that period, you were in fact not a casual employee
      `my claim for those entitlements` IS A NUMBER @desc What you claim for paid annual, personal or compassionate leave, public holidays, notice or redundancy pay for the period, in dollars
      `the proportion of the loading the court sets off` IS A NUMBER @desc The proportion of the loading the court sets off, from 0 to 1 (1 unless the court uses its s 545A(3) power to set off less)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`How much of my claim is left after the casual loading` MEANS
    IF `my job was described as casual` AND `I was not in fact a casual employee` AND `the loading I was paid` GREATER THAN 0
    THEN `the larger of` 0 (`my claim for those entitlements` MINUS (`the proportion of the loading the court sets off` TIMES `the loading I was paid`))
    ELSE `my claim for those entitlements`
